# pdf.xyz secure-worker ops-bundle — cron EXAMPLE (copy to /etc/cron.d/pdfxyz-ops-bundle).
#
# Assembles the operations evidence bundle on a schedule and lets the inert notifier wrapper
# persist the content-free artifact and map its documented composite exit code to an action.
# This is a TEMPLATE — adapt paths/user/cadence to your host; it is not a provisioned job.
# See docs/RUNBOOK.md (Phase 45).
#
# Composite exit ladder (worker:ops-bundle): 0 ok · 1 usage(the AGGREGATION is misconfigured,
# never a worker page) · 2 unknown · 3 warn · 4 critical. The wrapper re-raises 2/3/4 and
# swallows 1, so cron's MAILTO mails you only on a genuine worker incident.
#
# A heavier composite than the ops-check heartbeat — a 15-minute cadence is plenty for a handoff
# snapshot; keep worker:ops-check on its own tighter cadence for liveness.
#
# `/etc/cron.d` rows carry a user field (run as an unprivileged operator, never root).
# For a per-user crontab (`crontab -e`) drop the `pdfxyz` user column.
#
# Mail non-empty output to your operator address (a real MTA/relay is out-of-band):
MAILTO=REPLACE-ME-operator@example.invalid

# m h dom mon dow  user    command
*/15 * * * *       pdfxyz  /opt/pdfxyz/deploy/scheduler/ops-bundle-notify.sh

# Prefer to inline it instead of the wrapper? Then encode the SAME semantics yourself —
# alert on exit >= 2, and treat 1 as "the aggregation itself is broken", e.g.:
# */15 * * * * pdfxyz cd /opt/pdfxyz && pnpm -s worker:ops-check --web --json | pnpm -s worker:ops-bundle --json; c=$?; [ "$c" -ge 2 ] && your-notifier "ops-bundle exit $c"
